BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Finding Recovery Through Exercise Skills and Hope (FRESH)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T130000
UID:6a429fd1ed35742f00ff275a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This\n program helps people stay active\, make friends\, learn
  new skills\, and \nhave fun. It involves peer-led group activities which 
 can include crafts\, games\,\n walking\, yoga\, and more. Meet new folks\
 , build social connections\, and tell us\n about programs and services you
  would like to see in the neighbourhood!\nOn Tuesdays\, join us for crafts
  (11am-1pm) a walk (11:15am-1pm) and chair yoga (11:30am-12:30pm).\nSnacks
  and refreshments are provided for all participants. Ingredient lists will
  be available at the program.\nThis program is in collaboration with\nGer
 stein Crisis Centre's F.R.E.S.H. initiative\n.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Varies by program
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a429fd1ed35742f00ff275a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Personal Brand vs. Business Brand: Why Visibility Matters
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260721T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260721T200000
UID:6a2c8ba04407827f8aa49b71@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In today's business landscape\, people often connect with the 
 founder before they connect with the business. But how much of yourself sh
 ould you share? And what's the difference between building a personal bran
 d and building a business brand?\nThis workshop explores the role of visib
 ility\, storytelling\, and credibility in entrepreneurship. Participants w
 ill learn how personal and business brands work together to build trust\, 
 create opportunities\, and strengthen customer relationships.\nAll session
 s are interactive and participants are encouraged to bring questions relat
 ed to their own business or entrepreneurial goals.\nMeet the Resident\n: M
 elanie Cruickshank is a founder and brand-side operator with over 15 years
  of experience building\, launching\, and scaling beauty and personal care
  brands. As the founder of da lish cosmetics\, she led everything from pro
 duct development and retail strategy to e-commerce growth and national par
 tnerships. Known for her hands-on\, no-nonsense approach\, Melanie brings 
 a founder’s accountability and a deep understanding of what it takes to 
 build brands that perform in market.
LOCATION:S. Walter Stewart\, Meeting Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a2c8ba04407827f8aa49b71
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
