BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Connections
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T235959
UID:6a42b67e523d340f7c5b6f49@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, the works of artist Andrea Esla
 va are on display at the Yorkville Branch in an exhibit titled Connection
 s.\nArtist biography:\n"Andrea Eslava\, a visual artist based in Toronto\,
  explores abstraction through color\, form\, and emotion. Influenced by an
 cestral teachings\, her workinvites reflection on the space between the na
 tural landscape and presence. As the mother of a child with a brain injury
 \, she translates resilience and reconnection into paintings that reflect
  nature and transformation."\nArtist statement:\n"My work explores the con
 nections between the outer and the inner: nature and vibration\, where abs
 traction invites presence — a breath\, a memory beyond words. At its co
 re lies a dialogue between light and emotion\, connection and thought: syn
 apses\, echoing my daily routine of nurturing new neural pathways in my d
 aughter’s brain. Through color as symbol\, I embody stillness\, renewal\
 , resilience\, and a sense of belonging to something greater than ourselv
 es."\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibits Space\nprogram. Toronto 
 Public Library provides space for local artists to exhibit their work at l
 ocations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultur
 al interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewe
 d during\nYorkville\nbranch open hours.\nFree. All are welcome.\nImage cre
 dit: Artist: Andrea Eslava
LOCATION:Yorkville\, Art exhibit space on main floor.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a42b67e523d340f7c5b6f49
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dufferin/St. Clair Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260804T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260804T200000
UID:6958356a1e64afd01e52048f@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us the first Tuesday of each month to discuss books in a 
 fun and friendly atmosphere.\nCopies are available for pickup at the Duffe
 rin/St. Clair branch each month\, or place your own hold to have a copy se
 nt to your closest branch.\nJuly:\nNo Two Persons\nby Erica Bauermeister\
 nAugust:\nThe Covenant of Water\nby Abraham Verghese\nSeptember:\nPurity\
 nby Jonathan Frazen\nOctober:\nThe Edible Woman\nby Margaret Atwood\nNovem
 ber:\nSlammerkin\nby Emma Donoghue\nDecember:\nWhat We Can Know\nby Ian M
 cEwan
LOCATION:Dufferin/St.Clair\, Earlscourt Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6958356a1e64afd01e52048f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Classic Twists: The Savage Detectives
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260717T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260717T130000
UID:6a2c20874407827f8aa47afb@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:When it appeared in English translation in 2007\,\nThe Savage 
 Detectives\nestablished the Chilean writer Roberto Bolaño as one of the m
 ajor figures of 21st century literature. It tells the story of the Viscera
 l Realists\, a group of radical poets in Mexico City in the 1970s. But in 
 Bolaño's hands\, nothing is ever so straightforward. Inflected by noir ci
 nema\, experimental poetry\, dream-imagery\, and gumshoe detective novels\
 ,\nThe Savage Detectives\nfeatures dozens of narrators\, spans decades\, a
 nd takes the reader across continents\, ending in an insane pursuit of the
  founder of Visceral Realism through the Sonora Desert.\nIn this final ins
 talment of Toronto Public Library's virtual series on Classic Twists\, hos
 t Rebecca Panovka discusses Bolaño's masterpiece with Natasha Wimmer\, th
 e original English translator of\nThe Savage Detectives\,\nand novelist Ju
 lia Kornberg.\nABOUT THIS EVENT'S GUESTS:\nNatasha Wimmer\nis a translator
  of contemporary fiction and literary nonfiction from Spanish to English\,
  including the works of Robert Bolaño. She is a regular visiting lecturer
  at Princeton University and Columbia University\, and she has written rev
 iews and criticism for\nThe Believer\n\,\nThe New York Times\n\, and\nThe 
 Nation\n\, among other publications. She is the recipient of an NEA Transl
 ation Grant\, a PEN Translation Award\, and an Award in Literature from th
 e American Academy of Arts and Letters. She lives in Brooklyn\, New York\,
  with her husband and two children.\nJulia Kornberg\nis the author of the 
 novels\nBerlin Atomized\n(2024) and\nThe Parties\n(forthcoming). She is fr
 om Argentina and lives in New York City.\nRebecca Panovka\nis a founder an
 d editor of\nThe Drift\n\, a magazine of literature\, culture\, and politi
 cs. Her debut novel\,\nArchival Research\n\, is forthcoming from Norton.\n
 ***\nImportant information for accessing this live virtual event:\nYou mus
 t register for a free ticket via Eventbrite to gain access to this event.\
 nThis event will be presented in a Zoom Webinar. Once registered\, you wil
 l receive reminder emails from both Zoom and Eventbrite with links and ins
 tructions to access your unique Zoom Webinar link to watch this event.\nAt
  the time of the event\, you must be signed in to Zoom or Eventbrite with 
 the same email address that you used to book your ticket in order to acces
 s your unique event link. Unique event links can only be open on one devic
 e at a time.\n***\nThis event is made possible in part from a gift from th
 e Ann Thoburn Fund.\n***\nAccessibility at Toronto Public Library\nWe are 
 committed to making our content accessible to as many Torontonians as poss
 ible. Please call or email us if you are Deaf or have a disability and wou
 ld like to request accommodation to participate in this program. Please le
 t us know as far in advance as possible and we will do our best to meet yo
 ur request. At least three weeks' notice is preferred. Phone 416-393-7099 
 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a2c20874407827f8aa47afb
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Odyssey of the Voyager: Talk with Dr. Ilana MacDonald
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T193000
UID:6a21d7bb5459385f1bd26f2a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In 1977\, two spacecraft were launched from the Earth with the
  mission to explore the planets of our outer solar system. Dr. Ilana MacDo
 nald from the Dunlap Institute for Astronomy and Astrophysics will provide
  a presentation that follows their voyages through space\, learn about the
 ir discoveries and their current legacy. A short Q&A will follow.\nThis ev
 ent is hosted by the Merril Collection of Science Fiction\, Speculation an
 d Fantasy.\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toro
 nto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c
 )\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program re
 gistration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library han
 dles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tp
 l.ca\, 416-397- 5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Merril Collection Reading Room\, 3rd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a21d7bb5459385f1bd26f2a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Tamiko Potts
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260726T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260726T235959
UID:6a491afc48c98c3600a46655@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July the works of artist Tamiko Potts 
 are on display at Albion Branch.\nTamiko Potts (she/they) is a Toronto-bas
 ed\, queer\, Japanese Canadian multidisciplinary artist\, filmmaker and po
 et who has produced documentary television and Emmy-nominated animated se
 ries’ for the past two decades. Their work examines themes of identity\,
  resilience\, trauma and healing as well as their deep connection with na
 ture. Tamiko recently completed a documentary on their mother’s experien
 ce in the Japanese internment camps and is currently studying art psychot
 herapy at CIIAT.\n"My work is inspired by acrylic pouring and sumi-e paint
 ing. By combining these two mediums\, I explore the spontaneity and emotio
 nal release of poured acrylics contrasted against the quiet deliberation 
 and minimalism of traditional Japanese ink painting. Through the juxtaposi
 tion of elegant Japanese imagery over abstract pools of colour and motion
 \, I reflect on themes of identity and resilience. Each piece balances cha
 os and calm\, emotion and reflection\, honouring heritage while embracing
  change". - Tamiko Potts.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Spac
 e\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to exh
 ibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflec
 t the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe 
 exhibit can be viewed during Albion branch's open hours. Please check the\
 nAlbion branch\npage before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nPhoto cred
 it: Tamiko Potts\,\nIwashi (Sardines):\nTides of Abundance\, Oil\, acryl
 ic\, photography\, etc.: Acrylic\, Japanese brush painting\, Large
LOCATION:Albion\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a491afc48c98c3600a46655
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
