BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Understanding the Connection Between Sleep Apnea and Heart Disease
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260715T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260715T190000
UID:6a50205baa34e23600ee0ce2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Approximately 15 per cent of the general population suffer fro
 m sleep apnea\, affecting patients on a day-to-day basis by reducing sleep
  quality and resulting in daytime sleepiness\, decreased cognitive functio
 n\, and impaired functional capacity. In Ontario\, it is estimated that up
  to 80 per cent of those with sleep apnea remain undiagnosed. However\, sl
 eep apnea does more than just disrupt sleep. The frequent pauses in breath
 ing caused by sleep apnea can also strain your cardiovascular system and i
 ncrease the risk of high blood pressure and other heart diseases.\nWhat to
  Expect:\nUnderstanding normal sleep:\nWhat normal sleep should look like\
 nImpact of sleep apnea:\nUnderstand how sleep apnea can interrupt normal s
 leep causing poor sleep quality and daytime tiredness\nImpact of sleep apn
 ea on the heart:\nUnderstand how sleep apnea leads to increased strain on 
 the heart\nWhat can we do:\nUnderstand challenges to timely diagnosis in O
 ntario and how treatment of sleep apnea can potentially reduce the strain 
 on the heart\nBio:\nDr. Owen Lyons is an Assistant Professor of Medicine a
 t the University of Toronto and a Respirologist and Sleep Physician at Wom
 en’s College Hospital and the University Health Network\, and a scientis
 t at the Women’s College Research Institute. Dr. Lyons' research interes
 t is in Sleep Apnea and its interaction with other diseases including chro
 nic kidney disease and atrial fibrillation. He is also interested in diagn
 osis and management of sleep apnea in women\; despite becoming increasingl
 y common in women\, the condition is still traditionally considered a dise
 ase of middle-aged men. He is an advocate for improving access to sleep ap
 nea diagnosis and treatment in Ontario.\nAbout the Series\nBridging Minds:
  Health & Science Conversations is a partnership between the Toronto Publi
 c Library and Women’s College Hospital. Through monthly discussions led 
 by experts from across different clinical areas and scientific disciplines
 \, this series provides attendees with the opportunity to engage directly 
 with our topics and speakers and empowers communities to navigate health a
 nd science topics confidently\, inspiring a future of informed decision-ma
 king.\nLearn more:\nhttps://www.womenscollegehospital.ca/bridging-minds/\n
 Reserve Your Spot Today!\nThis virtual event is free to join but registrat
 ion is required.\nPlease register on Eventbrite\n.\nOnce registered\, you
 ’ll receive an email with details on how to join the session.\nWe can’
 t wait to see you there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a50205baa34e23600ee0ce2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
