BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fact or Fiction: Identifying and Combatting Misinformation and Di
 sinformation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260815T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260815T153000
UID:6a50fe23c7e02e3d00681ff2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you ready to separate fact from fiction in a world filled 
 with misinformation and disinformation? Join us for an interactive and ent
 ertaining program where we'll dive into the fascinating world of misinform
 ation\, exploring its origins\, impact\, and how to combat it effectively.
 \nThis program is perfect for curious minds who want to arm themselves wit
 h the skills needed to navigate the complex landscape of online informatio
 n responsibly and safely.\nRegistration is required via tpl.ca or by calli
 ng 416-396-8943.
LOCATION:Agincourt\, Learning Centre (LC)\, 1st floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a50fe23c7e02e3d00681ff2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Tamiko Potts
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260711T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260711T235959
UID:6a491afc48c98c3600a46646@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July the works of artist Tamiko Potts 
 are on display at Albion Branch.\nTamiko Potts (she/they) is a Toronto-bas
 ed\, queer\, Japanese Canadian multidisciplinary artist\, filmmaker and po
 et who has produced documentary television and Emmy-nominated animated se
 ries’ for the past two decades. Their work examines themes of identity\,
  resilience\, trauma and healing as well as their deep connection with na
 ture. Tamiko recently completed a documentary on their mother’s experien
 ce in the Japanese internment camps and is currently studying art psychot
 herapy at CIIAT.\n"My work is inspired by acrylic pouring and sumi-e paint
 ing. By combining these two mediums\, I explore the spontaneity and emotio
 nal release of poured acrylics contrasted against the quiet deliberation 
 and minimalism of traditional Japanese ink painting. Through the juxtaposi
 tion of elegant Japanese imagery over abstract pools of colour and motion
 \, I reflect on themes of identity and resilience. Each piece balances cha
 os and calm\, emotion and reflection\, honouring heritage while embracing
  change". - Tamiko Potts.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Spac
 e\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to exh
 ibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflec
 t the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe 
 exhibit can be viewed during Albion branch's open hours. Please check the\
 nAlbion branch\npage before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nPhoto cred
 it: Tamiko Potts\,\nIwashi (Sardines):\nTides of Abundance\, Oil\, acryl
 ic\, photography\, etc.: Acrylic\, Japanese brush painting\, Large
LOCATION:Albion\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a491afc48c98c3600a46646
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Excel Macros
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260727T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260727T153000
UID:6a072066177319280019ed9a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Understand the basic concept of Excel Macros\, be able to crea
 te a macro\, view/edit a macro\, and run/execute a macro\, including using
  shortcut key to macros. Basic Excel Knowledge and skills required.\nIf th
 e above class date does not fit for your busy schedule\, you may book an a
 ppointment with a librarian on one-on-one basis.\nInformation Collection N
 otice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto 
 Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, a
 nd 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registra
 tion\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles yo
 ur information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n
 \, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Maria A. Shchuka\, 2nd floor Learning Centre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a072066177319280019ed9a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
