BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Living Well Through Serious Illness\, End-of-Life & Loss
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260812T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260812T150000
UID:6a51198aaa34e23600ee3107@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Serious illness\, end-of-life\, and loss are challenging exper
 iences for individuals and families. Join a representative from Dorothy Le
 y Hospice to learn about the programs and services available to support pe
 ople through these important life transitions.\nThis session will explore:
 \nAn overview of the programs and services offered by Dorothy Ley Hospice\
 nHow these services support individuals and families through serious illne
 ss\, end-of-life\, and loss\nStories from staff and volunteers that highli
 ght the impact of this support on families\nDorothy Ley Hospice believes t
 hat no one should have to face serious illness\, end-of-life\, or grief al
 one. Learn more about the supports available when you or someone you care 
 about needs them.\nAbout the Presenter\nA representative from Dorothy Ley 
 Hospice will lead this presentation and share information about the organi
 zation's programs\, services\, and community supports\, along with stories
  that highlight the impact of hospice care for individuals and families.\n
 This program is presented by Toronto Public Library in collaboration with 
 the\nWest Toronto Ontario Health Team\n(OHT)\, a network of over 40 local 
 health and social service providers working together to improve the health
  and well-being of communities in West Toronto.\nDorothy Ley Hospice\nis a
  member organization of the West Toronto Ontario Health Team.\nYour Health
  Matters\nThis program is part of Toronto Public Library’s\nYour Health 
 Matters\ninitiative\, which offers trusted health programs and resources t
 o help people make informed decisions about their health. Learn more by ex
 ploring the\nYour Health Matters web page\n.\nReserve Your Spot Today!\nTh
 is free\, in-person event is open to everyone.\nRegistration is preferred\
 , but\ndrop-ins are welcome\n.\nRegister on Eventbrite to reserve your spo
 t\n.\nWe can't wait to see you there!
LOCATION:Runnymede
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a51198aaa34e23600ee3107
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Grown-ups Reading Children's Literature Book Club: Island of Blue 
 Dolphins
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260912T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260912T160000
UID:6a318f3190cc4e3a790e90fb@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for the monthly meeting of Osborne Collection Grown-up
 s Reading Children's Literature Book Club. It is  for adult readers who en
 joy children's books. Revisit favourite stories of your childhood or read 
 more or less known notable books for young readers for the first time.\nWe
  meet once a month on the first or second Saturday (2.30-4PM).\nSeptember 
 12: Island of the Blue Dolphins by Scott O’Dell\nOctober 3: A Series of 
 Unfortunate Events: The Bad Beginning by Lemony Snicket\nNovember 14: Borr
 owers by Mary Norton\nDecember 5: The BFG by Roald Dahl\nJanuary 9 (2027):
  Number the Stars by Lois Lowry
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Osborne Collection\, 4th floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a318f3190cc4e3a790e90fb
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Tamiko Potts
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260719T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260719T235959
UID:6a491afc48c98c3600a4664e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July the works of artist Tamiko Potts 
 are on display at Albion Branch.\nTamiko Potts (she/they) is a Toronto-bas
 ed\, queer\, Japanese Canadian multidisciplinary artist\, filmmaker and po
 et who has produced documentary television and Emmy-nominated animated se
 ries’ for the past two decades. Their work examines themes of identity\,
  resilience\, trauma and healing as well as their deep connection with na
 ture. Tamiko recently completed a documentary on their mother’s experien
 ce in the Japanese internment camps and is currently studying art psychot
 herapy at CIIAT.\n"My work is inspired by acrylic pouring and sumi-e paint
 ing. By combining these two mediums\, I explore the spontaneity and emotio
 nal release of poured acrylics contrasted against the quiet deliberation 
 and minimalism of traditional Japanese ink painting. Through the juxtaposi
 tion of elegant Japanese imagery over abstract pools of colour and motion
 \, I reflect on themes of identity and resilience. Each piece balances cha
 os and calm\, emotion and reflection\, honouring heritage while embracing
  change". - Tamiko Potts.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Spac
 e\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to exh
 ibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to reflec
 t the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThe 
 exhibit can be viewed during Albion branch's open hours. Please check the\
 nAlbion branch\npage before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nPhoto cred
 it: Tamiko Potts\,\nIwashi (Sardines):\nTides of Abundance\, Oil\, acryl
 ic\, photography\, etc.: Acrylic\, Japanese brush painting\, Large
LOCATION:Albion\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a491afc48c98c3600a4664e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Be Ready\, Toronto! Emergency Preparedness Workshop for Individual
 s and Households
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T193000
UID:6a19fa29caf93436005f3ef8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Emergencies can happen anywhere at any time. In Canada\, we fa
 ce numerous hazards\, including wildfires\, flooding\, heatwaves\, and ear
 thquakes. The Be Ready workshop aims to raise individual and household eme
 rgency preparedness.\nIn this free workshop facilitated by the\nToronto Em
 ergency Management Division of the City of Toronto\n\, in partnership with
  the\nCanadian Red Cross\n\, participants will learn about:\nThe potential
  hazards and risks in their area\nHow to create an emergency plan\nHow to 
 assemble an emergency kit\nThe importance of social and support networks d
 uring emergencies\nFree program. Register for a reminder. For more informa
 tion\, please contact the branch.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe per
 sonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library 
 under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The i
 nformation will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery
  and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information 
 can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981
 \, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall (1st floor)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a19fa29caf93436005f3ef8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Data Analytics Essentials Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260929T153000
UID:6a2afd9cf56bd86e00b61ddb@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Interested in learning about data analytics? Let's take a free
  online course together in a welcoming space!\nA\nlearning circle\nis a st
 udy group\, guided by library staff\, for people who want to take a free o
 nline course together in a supportive and welcoming environment.\nOrganiza
 tions all over the world recognize the value of analytics. Together\, we'l
 l learn about the essential tools used in this field.\nIn this 12-week lea
 rning circle\, we will work through the Data Analytics Essentials course f
 rom Cisco Networking Academy. We will explore the fundamental tools of a d
 ata analyst\, including how to:\ntransform\, organize and visualize data w
 ith spreadsheet tools like Excel\nquery data from a relational database us
 ing SQL\nimprove data presentations using business intelligence tools like
  Tableau\nWe will meet weekly\,\nin-person\,\nevery Tuesday afternoon\, Se
 ptember 15 - December 8\, 2-3:30 PM (12 sessions).\nParticipants are expec
 ted to commit to the duration of the course and attend sessions on a weekl
 y basis.\nLearners who complete the course will be able to:\nearn a digita
 l badge\ndownload a certificate of completion
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Meeting Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a2afd9cf56bd86e00b61ddb
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
