BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260713T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260713T190000
UID:69e95193b1165a41a6610870@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for a lively and fun discussion about the selected boo
 k of the month.\nThis book club has relocated to Main Street Branch during
  Dawes Road Branch's closure. New club members are welcome. Each month's s
 election will be available after the previous month's meeting.\nMay 11 :\n
 All the Light We Cannot See\nby Anthony Doerr\nJune 8 :\nDucks\nby Kate Be
 aton\nJuly 13 :\nThe Mistress of Nothing\nby Kate Pullinger\nAugust 10 :\n
 The Museum of Extraordinary Things\nby Alice Hoffman\nRegistration is requ
 ired. Please register in-person or by calling 416-393-7700.
LOCATION:Main Street\, Basement Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69e95193b1165a41a6610870
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Historical Fiction Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260806T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260806T200000
UID:69ea4dfc213814b1b524d6e1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you interested in historical people and events?\nJoin our 
 book club where we will read a wide range of historical fiction titles.\nJ
 uly 2:\nA Long Petal of the Sea\nby Isabel Allende\nPlease reserve your ow
 n copy or ask staff for help.\nAug 6:\nThe Nickel Boys\nby Colson Whitehea
 d\nCopies of the book will be available on July 2.\nSept 3:\nThe Night Wat
 chman\nby Louise Erdrich\nPlease reserve your own copy or ask staff for he
 lp.\nOct 1:\nWe Do Not Part\nby Han Kang\nPlease reserve your own copy or 
 ask staff for help.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, In the basement program room A
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ea4dfc213814b1b524d6e1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Let's Talk Comics!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260717T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260717T193000
UID:69ea6812defec6489cbfc51d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Whether you're an avid reader or a total beginner\, come join 
 us for a monthly discussion circle surrounding either a beloved comic book
  character or celebrated author in the comic world! Participants are welco
 me to select from any comics related to the monthly theme.\nRegistration 
 is optional\, drop-ins welcome.\nMonthly Themes:\nMay 15th:\nBatman\, char
 acter\nJune 19th:\nJames Tynion IV\, author\nJuly 17th\n: Supergirl\, char
 acter\nAugust 21st:\nGrant Morrison\, author\nSeptember 18th:\nSpider-Man\
 , character\nFuture dates to be determined.\nInformation Collection Notice
 :\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Publi
 c Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20
 (d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\
 , delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your in
 formation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 41
 6-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Parkdale\, Auditorium A
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ea6812defec6489cbfc51d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Creating Suspense in Crime and Mystery Fiction
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260831T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260831T200000
UID:69ecbe3bdefec6489cc01ea1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Award-winning crime writer Howard Shrier (\nMontreal Noir\n\;\
 nBuffalo Jump\n) shares techniques for building suspense in stories of cri
 me and mystery. With a short reading of a suspenseful passage from his new
  novel and by drawing on his experience as an author and creative writing 
 teacher\, Howard will show you how to create an outline that takes your ch
 aracters on the most challenging journey possible.\nInformation Collection
  Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toront
 o Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\,
  and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program regist
 ration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles 
 your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca
 \n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Lower Level
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ecbe3bdefec6489cc01ea1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Finding a Job with ACCES Employment
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260716T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260716T193000
UID:69ebdb675c04bc0fc0636d6c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Job Search Strategies & Networking\nThis session explores effe
 ctive job search techniques\, including online applications\, hidden job m
 arkets\, and networking strategies. Participants will learn how to leverag
 e platforms like LinkedIn and build meaningful professional connections.\n
 Participants are encouraged (but not required) to bring a copy of their re
 sume (digital or printed) for feedback.\nDrop-In. Space is limited.
LOCATION:Weston\, Program Room\, Basement Level
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69ebdb675c04bc0fc0636d6c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:100 years of the ups and downs of the bicycle: From the Bicycle Cr
 aze to Doug Ford
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260727T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260727T193000
UID:69f0e04a66c3387a9d082b64@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:​Cities around the world\, including Toronto\, are embracing
  the bicycle as a response to the climate crisis. This is not the first ti
 me the bicycle has come to our rescue\, proving itself a loyal friend duri
 ng times of crisis\, including the world wars and the COVID pandemic.\nIn\
 nWheeling Through Toronto\n\, author Albert Koehl takes the audience on a 
 130-year ride through the rich history of the bicycle in Toronto. By under
 standing how we got here\, we can begin mapping a way forward\, one in whi
 ch the potential of cycling is maximized.\nInformation Collection Notice:\
 nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public 
 Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d
 ). The information will only be used for event and program registration\, 
 delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your info
 rmation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-
 397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Lower Level
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/69f0e04a66c3387a9d082b64
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
